1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,750
Welcome to SciForge. This tour goes from a research question to
inputs, execution, and checkable deliverables.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:10,907
These are the new docs with real interface screenshots: a feature
walkthrough, not a continuous experiment recording.

3
00:00:12,033 --> 00:00:17,073
The home page shows each case's goal, key outputs, and
verification status, so you can judge if it fits your task.

4
00:00:17,073 --> 00:00:22,107
On the left: cross-scale data to evidence review. On the right: the
external model benchmark, kept marked experimental.

5
00:00:23,233 --> 00:00:27,470
Features are grouped as research workflow, workbench, and
connections and extensions, so you can start from your goal.

6
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:30,654
Switch to the workbench to learn sessions,
Agent collaboration, and tools.

7
00:00:30,654 --> 00:00:33,964
Connections and extensions covers the runtime, remote
resources, and specialized capabilities.

8
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:40,107
Start with the two virtual cell cases to see a task go from data to
evidence and report, then look up features as needed.

9
00:00:40,107 --> 00:00:44,897
Case one reviews existing outputs. Case two is external
model benchmarking, still an experimental flow.

10
00:00:46,033 --> 00:00:50,853
Model access provides the model; the runtime handles files
and tools. This read-only tutorial uses Codex.

11
00:00:50,853 --> 00:00:55,779
In Settings at lower left, open AI assistant, find the Codex
section, and check the command and sign-in status.

12
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:02,290
In the Codex section, change "can modify the working directory" to
"read files only", then restart and start a new session.

13
00:01:02,290 --> 00:01:06,523
Claude's plan mode is not equivalent to this sandbox. Saying "do
not modify files" does not guarantee isolation.

14
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:12,638
Use a clean Git workspace, run the status command on the page and
save the output; for a non-Git project, copy it first.

15
00:01:12,638 --> 00:01:16,122
Then open the local project, confirm the
directory and session, and send the task.

16
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:22,417
Paste the full read-only prompt into the input box. The task only
looks at top-level files and judges what the project is.

17
00:01:22,417 --> 00:01:26,795
A stated scope, expected output, and prohibitions help you
judge whether the result really met the request.

18
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:33,414
Watch file reads, tool calls, and command results. For an approval
request, check the actual action and target path.

19
00:01:33,414 --> 00:01:37,638
These screenshots show one read-only session, so you can
compare the three stages: send, run, finish.

20
00:01:38,767 --> 00:01:44,352
Check the answer and compare Git status again. It skips ignored files;
for strict acceptance, compare file lists and hashes.

21
00:01:44,352 --> 00:01:49,383
If something changed unexpectedly, stop, keep the scene, and use the
sandbox and troubleshooting pages to find the cause.

22
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:54,675
Case one covers the evidence relationships among transcription,
protein, pathways, and cell fitness after a perturbation.

23
00:01:54,675 --> 00:01:59,829
This real output is opened in the current dev build. Browsing is
verified; a full rerun still needs step-by-step checks.

24
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:06,598
Take stock in the Files panel: source data, rebuild scripts, derived tables,
figures, report. Each stage needs clear inputs and outputs.

25
00:02:06,598 --> 00:02:11,464
Before writing, check the plan and output directory, and
record code version, input hashes, and environment.

26
00:02:12,567 --> 00:02:17,758
The full prompt starts with a file inventory, then a plan,
quality checks, a review table, figures, and a dossier.

27
00:02:17,758 --> 00:02:22,322
It also requires keeping controls and missing data, stating
evidence limits, and listing external actions to confirm.

28
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:28,741
The inventory and quality report are stage-one deliverables. Check controls
were not lost and "not measured" is not read as "no response".

29
00:02:28,741 --> 00:02:33,421
The figure keeps each layer's coverage and missing status. Never fill
missing values with zeros; warnings go in the report.

30
00:02:34,567 --> 00:02:39,274
Link perturbations, target annotations, pathways, and responses in a
review table, with source and transformation per row.

31
00:02:39,274 --> 00:02:44,734
With only a ready-made derived table, limit the scope to reviewing
derived results, not redoing the raw-data analysis.

32
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:51,346
Figures come from audited derived tables, recording input, script,
parameters, and version, so result paths stay visible.

33
00:02:51,346 --> 00:02:55,745
If transcription and protein disagree in direction, keep the
result and check the time scale and panel coverage.

34
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:00,419
Cross-layer agreement helps form hypotheses, but one figure
cannot support a causal conclusion by itself.

35
00:03:00,419 --> 00:03:04,855
In the report, separate supported claims, candidate
explanations, and claims the data still cannot support.

36
00:03:05,967 --> 00:03:11,152
Finally, check file changes, link each key claim to data, method,
figure, and limits, and save versions and a checkpoint.

37
00:03:11,152 --> 00:03:16,111
The dossier should hold results, warnings, failed paths, and
rerun steps, so another researcher can keep checking.

38
00:03:17,233 --> 00:03:22,465
Case two is for researchers with an external prediction model.
SciForge organizes protocol and runs; the model predicts.

39
00:03:22,465 --> 00:03:27,438
It is marked experimental: full model runs and continuous verification
are unfinished. This shows the flow and requirements.

40
00:03:28,567 --> 00:03:34,188
Offline: open the reference answer only after predictions are sealed,
then score locally. Official: pre-check with current tools.

41
00:03:34,188 --> 00:03:38,694
Do not mix the truth and scoring claims of the two paths; a
passed pre-check is not an upload or an official score.

42
00:03:39,833 --> 00:03:44,873
Before the first prediction, fix the data split, metrics, aggregation
rules, stopping conditions, and what the model may see.

43
00:03:44,873 --> 00:03:49,866
Record code, weights, environment, device, and seed for every run, so
reruns never silently swap the model or preprocessing.

44
00:03:50,967 --> 00:03:55,484
First check the smallest slice: shape, gene order, background
labels, cells per condition, and value types.

45
00:03:55,484 --> 00:04:00,595
Exact requirements follow the official data package and CLI docs.
If any hard check fails, skip the full prediction.

46
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:06,148
After prediction, hash the files and keep the command, return
code, logs, and outputs in a separate run directory.

47
00:04:06,148 --> 00:04:10,793
The offline reference is for scoring only after sealing. A
rerun makes a new record and keeps its differences.

48
00:04:11,900 --> 00:04:16,349
Split the result into contract checks, metric components,
performance by background and perturbation, and interpretation.

49
00:04:16,349 --> 00:04:20,906
Keep failure conditions and limits, don't turn one total score into a
mechanism claim, then add the records to the dossier.

50
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:27,048
The workspace holds project files; the session holds the goal and
execution trail. Confirm project name and directory first.

51
00:04:27,048 --> 00:04:31,324
Open outputs and reference paths from the Files panel, so questions
can go back to real inputs and generating scripts.

52
00:04:32,433 --> 00:04:36,624
A task for the Agent states at least the goal, input scope,
deliverables, permissions, and stopping conditions.

53
00:04:36,624 --> 00:04:41,729
For complex tasks: check, plan, then execute and verify. If
incomplete, name the missing evidence and acceptance criteria.

54
00:04:42,833 --> 00:04:47,347
Check tool calls, terminal output, and return codes, then open the
Changes panel for additions, edits, and deletions.

55
00:04:47,347 --> 00:04:51,412
A task delivers actual files plus the evidence you checked,
and the final answer should help you find them.

56
00:04:52,567 --> 00:04:57,653
A file version pins an artifact, a checkpoint pins a stage, and the
evidence graph links claims to supporting data and methods.

57
00:04:57,653 --> 00:05:01,428
The dossier turns these records into a readable research
narrative; each object has its own job.

58
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:06,980
A dossier keeps the question, inputs, methods, results,
credibility, limitations, and how to rerun.

59
00:05:06,980 --> 00:05:11,520
In review, follow a conclusion to its figure, then script, data,
and run record, and confirm the chain has no gaps.

60
00:05:12,633 --> 00:05:16,607
For a task that repeats regularly, set its frequency,
workspace, inputs, and notification policy.

61
00:05:16,607 --> 00:05:20,485
Verify the smallest task by hand once, then check the
scheduled run's output, logs, and failure records.

62
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:25,647
When steps have dependencies and branches, use the flow canvas
to organize nodes, conditions, and human checks.

63
00:05:25,647 --> 00:05:30,060
Define recovery paths and stop conditions for failing nodes;
automated runs keep the task's original permissions.

64
00:05:31,167 --> 00:05:36,764
The Extension Center shows each extension's source, version, and status.
Find the capability, then verify it with a minimal task.

65
00:05:36,764 --> 00:05:40,691
"Enabled" only describes configuration; for a specific
task, still check the actual run result.

66
00:05:41,833 --> 00:05:47,676
Skills give specialized methods; MCP connects external tools and
data; GUI tools handle steps that must operate an app.

67
00:05:47,676 --> 00:05:52,529
Before use, understand scripts, dependencies, credentials, and
external effects; prefer clear, checkable tools.

68
00:05:53,633 --> 00:05:58,617
For a lab server or remote compute, set up an SSH
workspace through the remote resources wizard.

69
00:05:58,617 --> 00:06:03,296
Confirm the remote path and environment, keep the same inputs, outputs,
and run records, and check recovery after disconnects.

70
00:06:04,433 --> 00:06:09,206
The cloud collaboration page connects to and shows projects; what
you can use depends on the service and permissions.

71
00:06:09,206 --> 00:06:13,643
Before inviting members, syncing files, or uploading data, confirm
the target project, data content, and access scope.

72
00:06:14,767 --> 00:06:19,224
Read-only analysis and write tasks use different file scopes. When
approving, check the command, path, and external target.

73
00:06:19,224 --> 00:06:23,684
Keep credentials out of screenshots and logs; also check license,
de-identification, and sharing rules for research data.

74
00:06:24,833 --> 00:06:29,838
When something fails, check in order: workspace, runtime and model,
tools and permissions, external services, artifacts.

75
00:06:29,838 --> 00:06:34,476
When reporting a problem, include version, platform, reproduction
steps, a redacted log, and expected versus actual results.

76
00:06:35,633 --> 00:06:40,046
Now run the read-only task yourself from the five-minute
quickstart, then pick a case close to your research goal.

77
00:06:40,046 --> 00:06:44,459
Keep checking feature status and limits, and make every
analysis a deliverable you can open, check, and rerun.
